SQL Server Database Cheat Sheet (.NET & Student Registration Portal)
1. Data Types
int
Stores whole numbers.
Used for IDs, counts, credit hours, capacity.
Example:
StudentId INT
nvarchar(n)
Stores text (Unicode).
Supports English, Arabic, Chinese, emojis, etc.
n = maximum number of characters.
Example:
FirstName NVARCHAR(100)
bit
Boolean value.
1 = True
0 = False

Used for:

IsActive
IsPassed
RegistrationOpen
date

Stores only the date.

Example:

2026-08-09
time

Stores only the time.

Example:

09:30 AM
datetime2

Stores both date and time.

Example:

2026-08-09 09:30:15

Recommended over datetime.

decimal(p,s)

Stores decimal numbers.

Example:

Salary DECIMAL(12,2)
GPA DECIMAL(4,2)

p = total digits

s = digits after decimal point

2. NULL vs NOT NULL
Name NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL

The value is required.

Cannot be:

NULL
PhoneNumber NVARCHAR(30) NULL

The value is optional.

Can be:

NULL

In ER diagrams:

NN = NOT NULL
3. Primary Key (PK)

Uniquely identifies each row.

Properties:

Unique
Cannot be NULL
One PK per table

Example:

StudentId INT PRIMARY KEY
4. Foreign Key (FK)

Connects one table to another.

Example:

DepartmentId

inside Students references

Departments.DepartmentId

Relationship:

Departments
    |
    | 1
    |
    |∞
Students
5. Unique Key

Prevents duplicate values.

Example:

Email UNIQUE

Cannot have:

omar@gmail.com

omar@gmail.com
6. Composite Key

Primary key made from multiple columns.

Example:

PRIMARY KEY
(
StudentId,
CourseOfferingId
)

Used in junction tables.

7. Identity

Auto-increment.

Example:

StudentId INT IDENTITY(1,1)

Rows become

1

2

3

4

Automatically.

8. One-to-One Relationship

One record relates to exactly one record.

Example:

User
↓

Student

One user = one student.

9. One-to-Many Relationship

Most common relationship.

Example:

Department
     |
     |1
     |
     |∞
Students

One department has many students.

Each student belongs to one department.

10. Many-to-Many Relationship

Cannot be implemented directly.

Needs a junction table.

Example:

Students

∞

↓

Enrollments

↑

∞

CourseOfferings

One student can register for many courses.

One course can contain many students.

11. Junction Table

A table that converts a Many-to-Many relationship into two One-to-Many relationships.

Examples:

Enrollments

CourseDepartments

CoursePrerequisites

UserRoles

CourseOfferingInstructors
12. ER Diagram Symbols
PK = Primary Key

FK = Foreign Key

NN = NOT NULL

UQ = Unique

AI = Auto Increment